---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const linkClass = 'text-clay hover:underline hover:underline-offset-4';
---
<BaseLayout title="About the Compendium" description="How photos in Zack Miller's compendium are identified, and how it relates to iNaturalist.">
  <p class="text-sm"><a href="/compendium" class={linkClass}>&larr; Compendium</a></p>
  <h1 class="mt-4 text-2xl font-semibold">About the Compendium</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-ink-muted">
    The compendium is a curated collection of my photography: mostly living
    things, plus the occasional place worth remembering. It's meant to be
    browsed like an exhibition, not audited like a checklist.
  </p>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Identifications</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-ink-muted">
      Organisms are identified to the most specific level I can stand behind.
      Where possible, the photo is also posted to
      <a href="https://www.inaturalist.org" class={linkClass}>iNaturalist</a>,
      where other naturalists can review the identification.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 border-l border-line pl-4 text-ink-muted">
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">iNaturalist is the evidence; the compendium is the claim.</span>
        Community consensus informs each entry but never overwrites it
        automatically. Changes are reviewed before they appear here.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Disagreement means less specificity, not a guess.</span>
        When identifiers disagree, the entry is recorded at the level they
        share: <em>Quercus</em> sp. rather than a coin flip between two oaks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Unconfirmed isn't excluded.</span>
        Photos without a community-confirmed ID still appear, labeled as
        unconfirmed. Difficult subjects shouldn't disappear for being difficult.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Dissent is noted.</span>
        If I disagree with the community, my identification stands and the
        entry says why.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Cultivated plants stop at species.</span>
        Garden cultivars and topiary are recorded as their species, with the
        cultivar in the notes.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Why not just iNaturalist?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-ink-muted">
      If the goal were a verified species list, iNaturalist already does that
      better. It's a scientific record: every observation counts equally,
      photos exist to support an identification, and only living things
      qualify. The compendium is the other half.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 border-l border-line pl-4 text-ink-muted">
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Curation.</span>
        Every photo is ranked by tier. iNaturalist has no notion of a good photo.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Photography.</span>
        Full-resolution images with camera and lens details. The photo is the
        point, not the proof.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">More than organisms.</span>
        Places, several subjects in one frame, and the notes behind a shot.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Different ways to browse.</span>
        By quality, by date, and eventually by where each subject sits on the
        tree of life, including how it relates to everything else in the
        collection.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-6 text-ink-muted">iNaturalist verifies. The compendium exhibits.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
